<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lady of Elche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Dama de Elche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elche">Elche</a> is a limestone bust of a woman wearing elaborate jewellery and large wheel-shaped headdresses, carved by Iberian artists probably in the 4th century BCE. It was found in 1897 at La Alcudia near Elche, taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1941. It is now in the National Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche.jpg" alt="Lady of Elche" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luis Fernández García · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dama_de_Elche_-_MAN_-_20241220e.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
